</nav></header><main id="main"><div class="wrap"><section class="case-hero"><a class="back" href="index.html#work">← All selected work</a><p class="eyebrow">Veritas / GP Flow / Real estate investing · Product design</p><h1>Two roles. One investment platform.</h1><p class="case-intro">Designing connected workflows for sponsors managing investments and investors tracking their portfolios.</p><dl class="case-meta"><div><dt>Product</dt><dd>Real estate investment platform</dd></div><div><dt>My focus</dt><dd>UX/UI · User flows</dd></div><div><dt>Core complexity</dt><dd>Sponsor & investor roles</dd></div><div><dt>Deliverables</dt><dd>Dashboards · Forms · Workflows</dd></div></dl></section><div class="case-cover"><img src="assets/L2.png" alt="Investor portfolio and sponsor analytics dashboard designs for GP Flow" fetchpriority="high"></div><div class="case-layout"><aside class="case-toc" aria-label="On this page"><p>On this page</p><a href="#challenge">01 / The question</a><a href="#structure">02 / The structure</a><a href="#decisions">03 / Design decisions</a><a href="#outcome">04 / Deliverables & next steps</a></aside><article class="case-content"><section id="challenge"><h2>The tricky part was the roles.</h2><p>Sponsors need to manage offerings, contacts and distributions. Investors need to understand commitments, documents and the status of their money. Giving both roles the same interface would compete with the task each person came to complete.</p><p><strong>The design question:</strong> how can the platform serve both roles while making it clear which actions and information belong to each?</p></section><section id="structure"><h2>Start with what each role needs to do.</h2><p>The architecture separates sponsor and investor journeys, while keeping shared areas such as documents and transactions recognisable. The screens use an explicit role switch and consistent navigation patterns.</p><figure><button class="image-button" data-zoom aria-label="Expand image: Information architecture for sponsor and investor journeys"><img src="assets/b2b6.png" alt="Information architecture for sponsor and investor journeys" loading="lazy"></button><figcaption>A view of the platform structure and its connected workflows.<span>Click to expand ↗</span></figcaption></figure></section><section id="decisions"><h2>A few decisions that mattered.</h2><div class="decision"><small>Decision 01</small><h3>Put the portfolio into context.</h3><p>The investor overview combines a high-level portfolio summary with an asset table and distribution history. The hierarchy moves from overall position to individual investments.</p><p class="tradeoff"><strong>The trade-off:</strong> A summary makes the first read easier, but it cannot explain every financial detail. The asset-level view needs to remain easy to reach.</p></div><figure><button class="image-button" data-zoom aria-label="Expand image: Annotated investment dashboard design"><img src="assets/b2b2.png" alt="Annotated investment dashboard design" loading="lazy"></button><figcaption>Portfolio information grouped around summary, assets and activity.<span>Click to expand ↗</span></figcaption></figure><div class="decision"><small>Decision 02</small><h3>Keep role and task visible.</h3><p>The interface gives sponsors and investors distinct workspaces and a visible way to switch between them. Navigation follows the work performed in the current role.</p><p class="tradeoff"><strong>The trade-off:</strong> Role switching is useful for people wearing both hats. It also needs clear labels and permission handling so an action is never ambiguous.</p></div><figure><button class="image-button" data-zoom aria-label="Expand image: A selection of investment platform interface screens"><img src="assets/b2b4.png" alt="A selection of investment platform interface screens" loading="lazy"></button><figcaption>Connected screens across the investment workflow.<span>Click to expand ↗</span></figcaption></figure><div class="decision"><small>Decision 03</small><h3>Make status part of the workflow.</h3><p>Status labels, structured forms and document-related screens communicate where a task stands and what information belongs together.</p><p class="tradeoff"><strong>The trade-off:</strong> A status badge is only useful when its meaning is clear. Labels, error states and the next permitted action must agree.</p></div></section><section id="outcome"><h2>What I designed.</h2><ul class="deliverables"><li>Separate sponsor and investor experiences within a shared product structure.</li><li>Dashboard layouts that connect high-level summaries to detailed records.</li><li>Interface patterns for investment, document and distribution workflows.</li></ul><div class="learning"><p>The core design challenge is keeping people oriented as their role, information and next action change.</p></div><h3>What I’d test next</h3><p>Task completion for locating a document, understanding an investment’s status and completing a distribution workflow. I would also test whether users can identify their current role without prompting. These are proposed evaluation measures, not reported results.</p></section></article></div><a class="next-project" href="snapshot.html"><div><p class="eyebrow">Next project</p><h3>Snapshot Reviews</h3></div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div><section class="contact" id="contact"><div class="wrap contact-layout"><div><p class="eyebrow">A good place to start</p><h2>What are you<br>trying to solve?</h2><p>I’m open to product design roles and focused projects with teams building useful, thoughtful software.</p></div><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="mailto:tatheerulhassan799@gmail.com">Start a conversation <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div></section></main>
